          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/probability+theory"} probability theory
            %em stochastic map
            between sets
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            is a way of assigning numbers to elements
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x,y)
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ×
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\times Y
            to model a probabilistic transition from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            %em stochastic matrix
            or
            = succeed "," do
              %em transition matrix
          %p
            A generalization to arbitrary measurable spaces is called a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stochastic+kernel"} stochastic kernel
            (or
            = succeed ")." do
              %em Markov kernel
            %em stochastic map
            is used to denote a stochastic kernel.)
          %p
            If a stochastic map also preserves the uniform distribution, then it is called a
            = succeed "." do
              %em doubly stochastic map
            = succeed "." do
              %em doubly stochastic matrix
          %h2#definitions Definitions
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            be sets. A
            %strong stochastic map
            or
            %strong stochastic matrix
            from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            is a function
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo ×
                    %mi Y
                    %mo →
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:X\times Y\to[0,1]
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(y|x)
          %ul
            %li
              For all
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\in X
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi y
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y\in Y
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(y|x)
              is non-negative;
            %li
              For all
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\in X
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(y|x)
              are nonzero for only finitely many
              = succeed ";" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi y
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y\in Y
            %li
              For all
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\in X
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                        %mi Y
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sum_Y f(y|x) = 1
          %p
            The quantity
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(y|x)
            can be thought of as a transition
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/probability"} probability
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conditional+probability"} conditional probability
            of moving to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
            if the current state or current knowledge is
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
          %h3#composition Composition
          %p
            The product of stochastic matrices is again a stochastic matrix (and so they form a category, see below). Given stochastic matrices
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
            from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            to
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Z
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi g
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mi Y
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (g\circ f) (z|x) = \sum_Y g(z|y)\,f(y|x) .
          %p
            Its probabilistic interpretation is that the random transition from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
            and from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi z
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z
            are independent, as in a Markov process (hence their product is taken), and all intermediate states
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
            are mutually exclusive, so that the probabilities are summed over all
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
            = succeed "." do
              %em Chapman-Kolmogorov formula
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Markov+kernels"} Markov kernels
          %p
            As identity matrices are stochastic, stochastic matrices form a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
          %h3#measurepreserving_stochastic_maps Measure-preserving stochastic maps
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,p)
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Y
                  %mo ,
                  %mi q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (Y,q)
            be discrete
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/probability+spaces"} probability spaces
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p:X\to[0,1]
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi q
                  %mo :
                  %mi Y
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q:Y\to[0,1]
            which sum to one. A stochastic map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            is called
            %strong measure-preserving
            if for every
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y\in Y
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∈
                      %msup
                        %mi f
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\sum_{x\in f^{-1}(y)} p(x) = q(y) .
          %p
            (Compare this with the
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Markov+kernel#measurepreserving_kernels"} analogous notion for stochastic kernels
          %h3#stochastic_maps_from_deterministic_functions Stochastic maps from deterministic functions
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity"} identity
            function on a set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            defines a stochastic map as follows,
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi δ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi δ
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi x
                      %mo ′
                  %mrow
                    %mo {
                    %mrow
                      %mtable{:columnalign => "left left", :displaystyle => "false"}
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mn 1
                          %mtd
                            %mi x
                            %mo ′
                            %mo =
                            %mi x
                            %mo ;
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mn 0
                          %mtd
                            %mi x
                            %mo ′
                            %mo ≠
                            %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\delta(x'|x) = \delta_{x,x'} \begin{cases}
                  1 &amp; x'=x ; \\
                  0 &amp; x'\neq x
                  \\end{cases}
          %p
            for every
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi x
                    %mo ′
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x,x'\in X
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stochastic+map#categories_of_stochastic_maps"} below
          %p
            More generally, let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:X\to Y
            be a function. We can define the stochastic map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi δ
                    %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta_f:X\to Y
            as follows,
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi δ
                    %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi δ
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                  %mo =
                  %mrow
                    %mo {
                    %mrow
                      %mtable{:columnalign => "left left", :displaystyle => "false"}
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mn 1
                          %mtd
                            %mi f
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi x
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo =
                            %mi y
                            %mo ;
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mn 0
                          %mtd
                            %mi f
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi x
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo ≠
                            %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\delta_f(y|x) = \delta_{f(x),y} = \begin{cases}
                  1 &amp; f(x) = y ; \\
                  0 &amp; f(x) \ne y
                  \\end{cases}
          %p
            for every
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\in X
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y\in Y
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(x)
            with probability one. This construction induces a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
            from the category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
            to the categories of stochstic maps
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stochastic+map#categories_of_stochastic_maps"} below
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Markov+kernel#kernels_from_deterministic_functions"} analogous construction for stochastic kernels
          %h2#as_kleisli_morphisms As Kleisli morphisms
          %p
            A stochastic map can be equivalently written as a function
            %svg{:height => "26.58pt", :version => "1.2", :viewbox => "0 0 119.541 26.58", :width => "119.541pt", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/2000/svg", "xmlns:xlink" => "http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink"}
              %defs
